<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Meryey</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. c. 1208 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century-bce">13th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Meryey, son of Did, was a chief of the Libu who around 1208 BCE led a coalition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libyans">Libyans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-peoples">Sea Peoples</a>, including the Ekwesh, Shekelesh, Lukka, Sherden and Teresh, into the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile-delta">Nile Delta</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merneptah">Merneptah</a> defeated them at Perire, as recorded at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnak">Karnak</a> and on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merneptah-stele">Merneptah Stele</a>, and Meryey fled. The invasion is one of the first recorded movements of the Sea Peoples.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Meryey, whose name is also written Meghiey or Meghyey, was a Great Chief of the Libu in the late 13th century BCE and a contemporary of the pharaoh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merneptah">Merneptah</a>, who reigned from 1213 to 1203 BCE. His father had also been Great Chief of the Libu. Meryey had two brothers, Meshken and Kepper, and many wives, who went with him on campaign. He had six sons, all of whom were killed in the war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Meryey built a military alliance of his own people with the Meshwesh, the Lukka and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-peoples">Sea Peoples</a> known as the Ekwesh, Teresh, Shekelesh and Sherden, and led it against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile-delta">Nile Delta</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merneptah">Merneptah</a>'s 5th and 6th regnal years. His army is said to have numbered 40,000 men, and it had some success at first. The Great Karnak Inscription records that he fell upon the country of Tehenu with his bowmen, bringing his wife and children, and reached the western boundary in the fields of Perire.</p><p>The same inscription describes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merneptah">Merneptah</a> as enraged by the report. He addressed his court and dreamed that the god Ptah handed him a sword. After six hours of fighting, the invaders threw down their weapons, abandoned their baggage and dependants, and fled. Merneptah claimed to have killed 6,000 soldiers and taken 9,000 prisoners. The Egyptian list of spoils names Meryey's silver, gold and bronze vessels, his wife's furniture, his throne, his bows and arrows, and his oxen, goats and donkeys.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merneptah">Merneptah</a>'s records give a full account of Meryey's collapse on the battlefield as defeat drew near. They say that he fled alone by night with no plume upon his head, a reference to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrich-feather">ostrich feathers</a> that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libyans">Libyans</a> wore, while his women were taken, his grain plundered and his camp burned. His brothers turned against him, and on his return his own people spoke against him. The records present his fall as the curse of the gods of Memphis. After his defeat Meryey was replaced as chief by one of his brothers.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Meryey” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 1208 BCE their chief <b>Meryey</b>, son of Did, led a coalition including Sea Peoples against Merneptah, who defeated it at Perire and claimed over six thousand Libyans killed.</blockquote><a href="/state/libu">Libu</a></li></ul></section>
